#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ws-layout — REPTILE (re-tile; named by Dave 2026-09-03, a pair with Barbarian, the bar manager):
record which windows a workspace holds and how they are arranged, and put it back.

    ws-layout snapshot [ws]   record the desk: which windows, and the order they open in to rebuild it
    ws-layout restore  [ws]   open what is missing, close what does not belong, rebuild the arrangement
                              only when it differs from the recording
    ws-layout show     [ws]   print the recording's opening order and whether the desk matches it now
    ws-layout columns  [n]    spread this workspace's tiled windows into equal side-by-side columns
    ws-layout desks           every desk's recording as JSON, drawn as unit-square rectangles — the
                              Reptile panel's model (plus what is open but unrecorded, and the apps
                              a recording can name)
    ws-layout edit <ws> <op>  change a recording and print `desks` again. Ops: remove <i> ·
                              add <class> [<after> r|d] [title] · swap <i> <j> · share <i> <0.1-0.9>

All default to the workspace Dave is on. Bound to HYPER+S, HYPER+R and HYPER+3 (see bindings.lua).
⚠️ SAVE MOVED FROM N TO S ON 2026-08-11; HYPER+N is deliberately left unbound so old muscle memory
fails loudly rather than two keys doing one job.

THE RECORDING IS THE SPEC. Dave arranges a desk, presses HYPER+S, and from then on HYPER+R and every
login put it back. Nothing else lists what a desk holds: boot-apps.sh's hand-kept fleet and
tile-windows.sh's hand-written trees were both deleted on 2026-09-03 (`git show v1.57.17:` has them).

⚠️ NOTHING IS MOVED AFTER IT OPENS (Dave, 2026-09-03). The first version recorded every window's
exact rectangle and rebuilt it — float everything, re-tile, nudge each split twice — and that was
the "window dance" he saw at every login, even for a lone window that ended up exactly where it
started. A recording is now the OPENING ORDER: which window each new one is opened beside (dwindle
splits the focused window's cell), on which side (`preselect`), and what SHARE of the cell the
existing side keeps. Restore sets dwindle's default split ratio to that share right before each
window opens, so it appears at the recorded size, and waits for it to map before starting the next.
Shares are proportions, not pixels, so a recording gives the same look on a smaller screen. A desk
that already holds the right windows in the right arrangement is not rebuilt; if a split has been
dragged by hand since the recording, it gets one resize — the same move as SUPER+/- — and nothing
else moves.

⚠️ RESTORE CLOSES WINDOWS, so two rules are hard-coded rather than configurable:
  · an agent seat's terminal is NEVER closed (`NEVER_CLOSE`), because closing one leaves the seat
    running with nothing showing it — the exact fault fixed on 2026-08-11;
  · closing is `window.close`, which is a polite request the app can refuse, never a kill.
"""

